.TH CYAML_SERIALIZE_FLOW 3 "2026-09-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_serialize_flow \- serialize a DOM tree to flow-style YAML
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

char *cyaml_serialize_flow(cyaml node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Produce a compact, flow-style YAML text of the DOM subtree: a list
becomes [a, b, c], a dictionary becomes {key: value, ...}, and a scalar
obeys the same quoting rules as in cyaml_serialize().  The output is
normally one line.

The members of a dictionary go out in insertion order, and a float
goes out with a '.' in its mantissa, exactly as cyaml_serialize(3)
describes.

The rule for a tag is the same as in cyaml_serialize().  The function
writes a custom tag verbatim as "!<...>" and never writes a core-schema
tag, because a node only ever carries the core-schema tag of its own
type.  A byte that the URI characters of YAML 1.2 do not allow
literally inside "!<...>" becomes a percent escape here too, so a
second parse of the tag gives the identical string.  See
cyaml_serialize(3).

The rule for a dictionary key is also the same.  A key that is exactly
the canonical text of an integer or a float key goes out with no
quotes, and every key that a parse would read as another type or as
another text goes out double-quoted.  A key whose rendered text is
longer than 1024 bytes goes out as an explicit "? key: value" entry of
the flow mapping, for the reason that cyaml_serialize(3) gives.  The
rule for the decimal point of a float and the locale is the same as
there too.

The returned string comes from the allocator of the node.  Free it with
cyaml_serialize_free() when the tree used the default allocator, and
with cyaml_serialize_free_mp() and that same allocator when the tree
used a custom allocator: cyaml_serialize_free() hands the buffer to
plain free(), and plain free() never allocated a buffer that a custom
allocator gave.

node obeys the same 500-level nesting limit as in cyaml_serialize(3),
and this function also walks the tree with an explicit worklist instead
of by recursion, for the same reason; see the DESCRIPTION of that page.

A NULL node serializes in the same way as a CYAML_NULL node ("~").
This is a successful, non-NULL return, not a signal of failure, which
is where cyaml_clone(3) differs: it returns NULL for a NULL node.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A null-terminated string on the heap on success, or NULL when the
function runs out of memory or when node is nested more than 500 levels
deep.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_dictionary_first (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize_stream (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize_free (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize_free_mp (3)
